data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":352,"item_id":"fc_0bfe6e1fa8f24ccf016adb3d1c35a48191b0de4051dbec6007","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_candidate\\\":27,\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/inputs\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"N001\\\",\\\"N002\\\",\\\"N003\\\",\\\"N004\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/uses\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"N001\\\",\\\"N002\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r>0. The first direct input states that e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable, so E(r) is the ordinary finite integral from D002. The second direct input states that E is differentiable at r and E′(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/uses\\\",\\\"value\\\":[\\\"N003\\\",\\\"N004\\\"]},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"The third direct input gives E′(r)=2∫u_t(x,r)·(u_{tt}(x,r)−Δ_xu(x,r))dx. The fourth states that this integrand is zero for every x, so its integral is zero. Hence E′(r)=0. Together these facts establish integrability, differentiability, and the claimed conservation law for arbitrary r>0. All dot products are Euclidean in ℝ^m and the spatial derivative sum is the Frobenius square specified by D001.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/applications\\\",\\\"value\\\":[]}]}\"}"}

